1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
They're one of the most dangerous and destructive animals on Earth,

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:11,000
spreading deadly diseases and terrifying humankind for centuries.

3
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:14,000
One's actually lurched at me, so you've got to be really careful.

4
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:17,000
But they've also saved countless lives,

5
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:21,000
helping to push the limits of biological science and technology.

6
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:23,000
And despite all we've done to get rid of them,

7
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:25,000
they're the ultimate survivors.

8
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:29,000
There were so many rodents, and I felt like I was spoiling their party.

9
00:00:29,001 --> 00:00:33,340
Now, the untold story of rats on Modern Marvels.

10
00:00:46,040 --> 00:00:47,300
New York City.

11
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:51,760
By day, more than 8 million people bustle through the streets.

12
00:00:52,460 --> 00:00:54,600
But by night, rats rule.

13
00:00:55,680 --> 00:00:56,780
Estimates vary wildly.

14
00:00:56,781 --> 00:01:00,160
from 250,000 to close to 100 million.

15
00:01:02,280 --> 00:01:04,920
Terminix technical specialist Bob Young's guess

16
00:01:04,921 --> 00:01:06,500
is towards the high end.

17
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:10,060
I've walked into rooms where I actually felt out of place

18
00:01:10,061 --> 00:01:12,180
because there were so many rodents looking at me,

19
00:01:12,181 --> 00:01:14,240
and I felt like I was spoiling their party.

20
00:01:15,580 --> 00:01:17,780
Bob is no stranger to the danger.

21
00:01:18,520 --> 00:01:21,080
He's been in the rat business for more than 14 years.

22
00:01:21,700 --> 00:01:24,040
When I hear people tell me about the largest rodent

23
00:01:24,041 --> 00:01:25,800
they've ever seen, they're really fishtails.

24
00:01:26,340 --> 00:01:29,480
They typically range maybe half a pound to a pound,

25
00:01:29,960 --> 00:01:33,840
but I can tell you by no means have I seen three, four, five-pound rodents.

26
00:01:34,140 --> 00:01:37,120
And you'll hear people say that their eyes are glowing and that they're evil.

27
00:01:37,660 --> 00:01:40,680
Truly, people have a fear, an innate fear of rodents.

28
00:01:41,560 --> 00:01:44,680
We're in Queens, New York, going to an account,

29
00:01:45,080 --> 00:01:47,820
and they've been experiencing problems with Norway rats.

30
00:01:49,520 --> 00:01:53,100
We basically treat it, for lack of a better description, as a crime scene,

31
00:01:53,101 --> 00:01:56,060
and we're looking for evidence of rodent activity.

32
00:01:56,700 --> 00:02:00,280
And what we found here was several droppings throughout the building

33
00:02:00,281 --> 00:02:03,280
and also gnawing marks.

34
00:02:03,860 --> 00:02:06,020
The workshop is a rat's paradise.

35
00:02:06,600 --> 00:02:09,980
Plenty of food, water, and hiding places.

36
00:02:12,480 --> 00:02:16,580
As darkness falls, the nocturnal animals are starting to stir.

37
00:02:23,100 --> 00:02:36,540
They're very cautious, especially when people are around.

38
00:02:36,541 --> 00:02:39,940
But in the same token, you do hear the squeals of the pups.

39
00:02:39,941 --> 00:02:43,260
So we do have a couple of families here.

40
00:02:43,261 --> 00:02:46,860
The cunning creatures are creeping all around.

41
00:02:46,861 --> 00:02:48,100
It's a near infestation.

42
00:02:48,101 --> 00:02:52,500
Oh, yes.

43
00:02:52,501 --> 00:02:54,260
Here we go.

44
00:02:54,261 --> 00:02:56,940
Now look at this here.

45
00:02:56,941 --> 00:02:57,780
Now we have activity.

46
00:02:57,781 --> 00:02:59,580
I just saw one come out here.

47
00:02:59,581 --> 00:03:02,500
So they're actually living in this little protected area.

48
00:03:02,501 --> 00:03:05,940
But as you can see, the challenge is the clutter.

49
00:03:05,941 --> 00:03:09,220
Scattered throughout the site are numerous bait stations

50
00:03:09,221 --> 00:03:11,620
and traps with peanut butter and nesting materials,

51
00:03:11,621 --> 00:03:15,160
such as insulation, to entice the pests.

52
00:03:15,161 --> 00:03:16,800
And each night, Terminex technicians

53
00:03:16,801 --> 00:03:19,600
are collecting bagfuls of rats.

54
00:03:19,601 --> 00:03:20,920
Based on what we're seeing right now,

55
00:03:20,921 --> 00:03:23,120
We're definitely in the area of 50 plus rodents

56
00:03:23,121 --> 00:03:24,660
because they're running all over.

57
00:03:24,661 --> 00:03:27,320
And we know we have established colonies in different areas,

58
00:03:27,321 --> 00:03:28,760
different nesting sites.

59
00:03:28,761 --> 00:03:31,020
But in speaking with the owner, at this point,

60
00:03:31,021 --> 00:03:34,340
he's looking to possibly relocate.

61
00:03:34,341 --> 00:03:38,160
But this infestation is not an anomaly.

62
00:03:38,161 --> 00:03:40,300
Rats invade more than 21 million homes

63
00:03:40,301 --> 00:03:42,700
in the United States each year.

64
00:03:42,701 --> 00:03:47,080
A group of rats is called the mischief, which is a perfect

65
00:03:47,081 --> 00:03:48,680
name if you think about it.

66
00:03:48,681 --> 00:03:51,840
This is a group of animals that will chew through electrical cords,

67
00:03:52,100 --> 00:03:56,040
will undermine foundation, will eat our food, will drink our water.

68
00:03:57,100 --> 00:04:01,300
Rats eat or contaminate more than 20% of the world's food crops

69
00:04:01,301 --> 00:04:05,580
and cause $19 billion damage in the United States annually.

70
00:04:07,160 --> 00:04:09,740
A single rat sheds a million hairs a year,

71
00:04:10,600 --> 00:04:14,620
so eliminating all contamination in processed foods is virtually impossible.

72
00:04:14,621 --> 00:04:15,806
In fact, the U.S.

73
00:04:15,807 --> 00:04:22,640
Food and Drug Administration allows an average of two rodent hairs per 100 grams of peanut butter.

74
00:04:24,620 --> 00:04:28,340
Beyond spoiling our food supply, rats damage our structures.

75
00:04:29,280 --> 00:04:34,160
The name rodent appropriately comes from the Latin word rodere, meaning to gnaw.

76
00:04:35,640 --> 00:04:41,420
Rats will gnaw on wood, metal, concrete, electrical cables, virtually anything.

77
00:04:41,421 --> 00:04:48,420
In fact, the number of house fires caused by rats is estimated to be as high as 25%.

78
00:04:48,421 --> 00:04:53,240
Rat teeth are stronger than iron, copper, and lead.

79
00:04:53,900 --> 00:04:57,600
Their jaws can exert a force of 7,000 pounds per square inch.

80
00:04:59,380 --> 00:05:01,160
That's the same force as a crocodile.

81
00:05:02,620 --> 00:05:07,360
While their gnawing routine may give you the willies, for the rats, it's a fact of life.

82
00:05:08,540 --> 00:05:11,260
Gnawing keeps the teeth short and razor sharp.

83
00:05:11,420 --> 00:05:17,260
That's because their chisel-like front teeth continuously grow about 2.5 millimeters per

84
00:05:17,261 --> 00:05:19,340
week.

85
00:05:19,341 --> 00:05:21,420
That's almost 5 inches a year.

86
00:05:21,421 --> 00:05:27,180
In fact, rats with misaligned jaws have actually pierced their own brain cases with their own

87
00:05:27,181 --> 00:05:30,540
rapidly growing teeth.

88
00:05:30,541 --> 00:05:33,380
Chew on that for a moment.

89
00:05:33,381 --> 00:05:37,860
With so many rats causing so much mischief, it's no surprise that professional pest control

90
00:05:37,861 --> 00:05:40,700
is a $6 billion industry in the U.S. alone.

91
00:05:42,900 --> 00:05:46,020
And companies like Orkin are never short on business.

92
00:05:48,220 --> 00:05:51,900
Each Orkin man must train through their $10 million center

93
00:05:51,901 --> 00:05:53,480
located in Atlanta, Georgia,

94
00:05:54,220 --> 00:05:56,380
complete with a truly one-of-a-kind abode.

95
00:05:57,260 --> 00:06:00,020
We're here at the Orkin Residential Training House,

96
00:06:00,021 --> 00:06:02,160
and it's a little different than most houses

97
00:06:02,161 --> 00:06:04,620
because we want to have all pests welcomed.

98
00:06:04,621 --> 00:06:09,500
Here, technicians learn what attracts rats into a home.

99
00:06:10,460 --> 00:06:14,260
It's the perfect place to simulate some of the telltale signs of a rat infestation.

100
00:06:15,760 --> 00:06:21,880
Back here at the back, you can actually see droppings here, urine that's along this side.

101
00:06:22,240 --> 00:06:23,860
This is where you'd find grease marks.

102
00:06:24,240 --> 00:06:28,320
In fact, running back and forth, what rats have is a lot of grease in their coat

103
00:06:28,321 --> 00:06:32,360
and also some nerve hairs that actually help them feel their way along.

104
00:06:32,840 --> 00:06:38,300
And so what's going on is they move along, they urinate, they defecate, and the grease gets on the wall.

105
00:06:38,740 --> 00:06:43,020
In fact, their bladders are not very good, and so everywhere they go, they're urinating.

106
00:06:43,540 --> 00:06:47,160
In fact, we've actually found that a rat can urinate a gallon in a year.

107
00:06:47,680 --> 00:06:50,920
And it doesn't take much for these greasy rodents to enter your home.

108
00:06:52,800 --> 00:06:57,380
A hole as small as the diameter of a pencil is all a rat needs to gnaw his way in.

109
00:06:57,381 --> 00:07:03,560
Once a rat can get its head through the hole, the body will ultimately follow.

110
00:07:04,560 --> 00:07:07,640
That's because of the rodent's long and flexible cylindrical skeleton.

111
00:07:09,460 --> 00:07:15,200
The resilient rodent can even climb right up your bathroom pipes and out of your toilet.

112
00:07:16,820 --> 00:07:20,140
From the plumbing to the attic to the crawl space,

113
00:07:20,840 --> 00:07:23,900
rats will take advantage of all the nooks and crannies of your home.

114
00:07:23,901 --> 00:07:31,380
In fact, some experts say that at any given time, you're never more than 10 feet away from a rat.

115
00:07:32,120 --> 00:07:36,900
Along with that, if you corner a Norway rat, they'll actually try to defend themselves,

116
00:07:37,040 --> 00:07:40,720
and occasionally one has actually lurched at me. So you've got to be really careful.

117
00:07:42,400 --> 00:07:47,100
Fortunately, we're smarter than rats, so we've devised a variety of methods to capture them.

118
00:07:48,020 --> 00:07:52,660
The traditional snap trap is one of the most recognized devices when it comes to rodent control.

119
00:07:53,900 --> 00:07:57,280
But while a mouse trap can give you a painful sting,

120
00:07:57,920 --> 00:08:00,000
a rat trap can easily break your finger.

121
00:08:00,720 --> 00:08:03,640
It's really important that when you're using these snap traps,

122
00:08:03,820 --> 00:08:06,280
you have material that attracts the rat in.

123
00:08:06,440 --> 00:08:08,140
If they're eating dog food, you can use that.

124
00:08:08,280 --> 00:08:10,040
Sometimes squid works really well.

125
00:08:11,040 --> 00:08:13,780
While the low-tech snap trap has changed very little

126
00:08:13,781 --> 00:08:15,940
since it was first introduced in 1894,

127
00:08:16,460 --> 00:08:19,580
other devices have taken a more high-tech approach.

128
00:08:19,581 --> 00:08:24,380
Case in point, the Victor electronic trap.

129
00:08:25,380 --> 00:08:30,420
To set this trap, all you need is a dab of their favorite snack and 4C batteries.

130
00:08:31,260 --> 00:08:33,000
That's good for 50 rats.

131
00:08:34,840 --> 00:08:38,740
As soon as the unknowing rat enters the trap, it steps on metal plates

132
00:08:38,741 --> 00:08:42,940
that trigger a 7,500-volt shock for two minutes.

133
00:08:44,080 --> 00:08:48,400
That's necessary, since rats can restart their hearts after an electric shock.

134
00:08:49,580 --> 00:08:54,980
Finally, a light flashes green to alert you to the kill.

135
00:08:54,981 --> 00:09:00,140
Other methods of eradication rely on the rats highly developed sense of taste.

136
00:09:00,141 --> 00:09:06,180
Rats can detect tastes as small as 0.5 parts per million. That's about the

137
00:09:06,181 --> 00:09:12,140
equivalent of two grains of salt in a pound of peanut butter. Rats depend on

138
00:09:12,141 --> 00:09:17,180
this keen sense of taste to avoid poisons. Even the slightest taste can be

139
00:09:17,181 --> 00:09:20,180
because rats lack muscle strength in their esophagus,

140
00:09:20,181 --> 00:09:23,180
preventing them from being able to vomit.

141
00:09:23,181 --> 00:09:25,180
The bottom line?

142
00:09:25,181 --> 00:09:29,180
Making an effective rodenticide is particularly complicated.

143
00:09:29,181 --> 00:09:32,180
At the 80,000-square-foot Haco Incorporated plant

144
00:09:32,181 --> 00:09:35,180
in Randolph, Wisconsin, technicians can whip up

145
00:09:35,181 --> 00:09:39,180
100,000 pounds of tasty rat baits in a day.

146
00:09:39,181 --> 00:09:42,180
What we're making today is what we call Rodex block.

147
00:09:42,181 --> 00:09:46,180
Rodex block is utilized primarily in very wet areas

148
00:09:46,181 --> 00:09:49,666
wet areas where one wants to use a wax-based product.

149
00:09:49,667 --> 00:09:52,100
So we'll melt this with slab wax into

150
00:09:52,101 --> 00:10:00,100
a liquid mixture in about 200 degrees and then we'll add the grain product to it and get a nice

151
00:10:00,101 --> 00:10:04,340
consistent mixture of that and then we'll add our pre-mix to it which is going to be containing our

152
00:10:04,341 --> 00:10:07,943
active so we can get a nice dispersion of about 50 parts per million.

153
00:10:07,944 --> 00:10:09,300
The important part of this

154
00:10:09,301 --> 00:10:14,900
process is that we don't burn, we don't scald, we maintain a quality of temperature very much like

155
00:10:14,901 --> 00:10:17,740
in the food industry, because rodents,

156
00:10:17,840 --> 00:10:19,440
while they're picky on some things,

157
00:10:19,540 --> 00:10:22,080
we've got to make sure that they're attracted to our baits.

158
00:10:22,180 --> 00:10:26,340
We basically are raft chefs, and we were pretty proud of it.

159
00:10:26,440 --> 00:10:27,880
And like most chefs,

160
00:10:27,980 --> 00:10:32,920
Hako won't divulge all the details of their secret recipe.

161
00:10:33,020 --> 00:10:37,660
Once blended to perfection, the batch of bait must be cooled.

162
00:10:37,760 --> 00:10:42,060
The wax mixture in this stage is about 160 degrees Fahrenheit.

163
00:10:42,061 --> 00:10:44,760
We're putting it onto a metal belt that is cooled from

164
00:10:44,860 --> 00:10:47,300
underneath with a cool water basis.

165
00:10:47,400 --> 00:10:50,100
Then it brings it down to about 70 degrees by the time we get

166
00:10:50,200 --> 00:10:52,600
to the end of the line.

167
00:10:52,700 --> 00:10:55,480
The slow cooling process ensures the wax and the grain

168
00:10:55,580 --> 00:10:58,640
won't separate and maintains a consistent level of poison

169
00:10:58,740 --> 00:11:00,920
throughout the bait.

170
00:11:01,020 --> 00:11:05,020
What we're doing now is taking the cooled sheets of the wax

171
00:11:05,120 --> 00:11:08,880
and elevating them to a mixing station that is now going to be

172
00:11:08,980 --> 00:11:11,260
processing through essentially what we have

173
00:11:11,261 --> 00:11:15,160
have is an industrial Play-Doh machine.

174
00:11:15,161 --> 00:11:17,300
The active ingredient in this final product

175
00:11:17,301 --> 00:11:20,200
is an anticoagulant that works by inhibiting the blood's

176
00:11:20,201 --> 00:11:22,440
ability to clot.

177
00:11:22,441 --> 00:11:24,540
This damages the rat's blood vessels

178
00:11:24,541 --> 00:11:27,340
and creates internal bleeding, resulting in death

179
00:11:27,341 --> 00:11:31,540
within four to 10 days of feeding.

180
00:11:31,541 --> 00:11:34,620
While rat poison may help eliminate your rat problem,

181
00:11:34,621 --> 00:11:37,560
experts agree this rodent is here to stay.

182
00:11:37,561 --> 00:11:40,580
Eradication does not happen in rat population.

183
00:11:40,581 --> 00:11:43,720
They are certainly an animal that has survived through millennia

184
00:11:43,721 --> 00:11:47,200
and will continue to do so in harmony with us one way or the other.

185
00:11:47,380 --> 00:11:50,400
So we have to learn how to control them as well as to live with them.

186
00:11:51,420 --> 00:11:53,780
Since we can't fully rid the world of rats,

187
00:11:54,640 --> 00:11:58,680
perhaps it's time we face our fears and get to know these rodents a little bit better.

188
00:11:59,680 --> 00:12:01,720
And when it comes to the rat's awesome abilities,

189
00:12:02,460 --> 00:12:04,480
we've only scratched the surface.

190
00:12:06,480 --> 00:12:09,380
Rats eat about one-tenth their body weight each day.

191
00:12:09,381 --> 00:12:12,020
If that held true for people, the average adult

192
00:12:12,120 --> 00:12:15,480
would need about 15 pounds of food a day.

193
00:12:15,580 --> 00:12:17,620
Rats will return on Modern Marvels.

194
00:12:24,920 --> 00:12:26,960
Technicians at the Taconic Breeding Facility

195
00:12:27,060 --> 00:12:30,460
in Germantown, New York, must go through this rigorous routine

196
00:12:30,560 --> 00:12:32,240
every day.

197
00:12:32,340 --> 00:12:35,400
It's not for their own safety, but rather the safety

198
00:12:35,500 --> 00:12:38,200
of the multitude of rats in their care.

199
00:12:38,201 --> 00:12:43,700
Taconica has been in the business of producing rats and mice for the use of research and

200
00:12:43,701 --> 00:12:46,240
testing for over 50 years now.

201
00:12:46,241 --> 00:12:49,720
Behind me you'll see a number of isolated barrier units.

202
00:12:49,721 --> 00:12:55,120
At the site there are approximately 80 of these rooms and we have over 10,000 cages

203
00:12:55,121 --> 00:12:56,120
of rats.

204
00:12:56,121 --> 00:13:01,200
It's one of the largest and most innovative maximum barrier rat and mouse breeding

205
00:13:01,201 --> 00:13:03,780
facilities in the world.

206
00:13:03,781 --> 00:13:09,620
That means the rats are kept secluded from any and all infectious agents.

207
00:13:09,621 --> 00:13:15,420
Rats have been absolutely pivotal in medical research and science, so it's absolutely imperative

208
00:13:15,421 --> 00:13:18,380
that these animals be kept at the highest quality.

209
00:13:18,381 --> 00:13:24,020
We control the air, the water, and the personnel and materials that enter to protect the animals.

210
00:13:24,021 --> 00:13:26,640
I've been here for four years.

211
00:13:26,641 --> 00:13:27,960
I really love the rats.

212
00:13:27,961 --> 00:13:29,980
I love their personalities.

213
00:13:29,981 --> 00:13:36,040
My favorite part about the job is knowing that what we are doing is helping find cures

214
00:13:36,041 --> 00:13:37,820
for diseases.

215
00:13:37,821 --> 00:13:43,540
The animals themselves are actually bred and hand raised in germ-free bubbles called

216
00:13:43,541 --> 00:13:45,300
notobiotic isolators.

217
00:13:45,301 --> 00:13:54,500
The notobiotic center is Taconic's home base for every rat type.

218
00:13:54,501 --> 00:13:58,400
Whether a researcher is looking for albino, black hooded,

219
00:13:58,401 --> 00:14:01,100
or the more traditional brown rat,

220
00:14:01,101 --> 00:14:03,640
Tacona can fulfill their rodent needs.

221
00:14:03,641 --> 00:14:06,740
We have 11 different lines of rats.

222
00:14:06,741 --> 00:14:10,520
Some are outbred, which actually mimic the human population,

223
00:14:10,521 --> 00:14:12,620
where they are genetically diverse.

224
00:14:12,621 --> 00:14:14,720
Another type called inbred strains

225
00:14:14,721 --> 00:14:18,260
has been purposely bred for many generations

226
00:14:18,261 --> 00:14:21,060
to maximize the genetic identity.

227
00:14:21,061 --> 00:14:24,220
So it's like having a room full of almost identical twins.

228
00:14:24,900 --> 00:14:29,720
And then there are some very unique rats, like the so-called nude rat.

229
00:14:30,560 --> 00:14:34,040
Because of its sparse hair coat and abnormal immune system,

230
00:14:34,560 --> 00:14:39,760
the nude rat can actually grow human tumors for scientists to use in testing treatments.

231
00:14:40,140 --> 00:14:44,160
We have a lot of pharmaceutical companies that are purchasing our rats,

232
00:14:44,161 --> 00:14:50,120
as well as biotechnology companies, academic organizations, and the National Institutes of Health.

233
00:14:51,060 --> 00:14:53,060
Many of us would be loath to take a job

234
00:14:53,061 --> 00:14:55,060
in a rat breeding facility.

235
00:14:57,060 --> 00:14:59,060
That may be because rats have been vilified

236
00:14:59,061 --> 00:15:01,060
for hundreds of years.

237
00:15:01,061 --> 00:15:03,060
Is the bad rap justified?

238
00:15:03,061 --> 00:15:06,060
Let's take a closer look at the furry little critters.

239
00:15:08,060 --> 00:15:11,060
Rats are mammals that belong to the most successful animal

240
00:15:11,061 --> 00:15:13,060
group on Earth, the rodents.

241
00:15:13,061 --> 00:15:17,060
This group includes chipmunks, squirrels, and mice,

242
00:15:17,061 --> 00:15:19,060
to name a few.

243
00:15:19,061 --> 00:15:23,060
The genus Rattus includes about 50 species,

244
00:15:23,061 --> 00:15:26,060
but two in particular found their way into our homes.

245
00:15:26,061 --> 00:15:30,060
The two main ones that we deal with here in the United States

246
00:15:30,061 --> 00:15:32,060
are the nori rat and the roof rat.

247
00:15:32,061 --> 00:15:34,060
The nori rat is quite a bit bigger,

248
00:15:34,061 --> 00:15:37,060
weighs between a pound, maybe a pound and a half,

249
00:15:37,061 --> 00:15:41,060
kind of small ears, small eyes, and somewhat of a blunt nose.

250
00:15:41,061 --> 00:15:45,060
On the other hand, the roof rat maybe only weighs about a half a pound,

251
00:15:45,061 --> 00:15:47,060
but has really long ears.

252
00:15:47,061 --> 00:15:52,000
ears, but one thing really helps you identify it is the tail.

253
00:15:52,001 --> 00:15:56,060
It has a very, very long tail.

254
00:15:56,061 --> 00:15:59,800
Roof rats are native to the forests of Southeast Asia.

255
00:15:59,801 --> 00:16:02,500
From there, they spread across the continent and then traveled as

256
00:16:02,501 --> 00:16:07,940
stowaways on ships carrying soldiers home from the Crusades to Europe.

257
00:16:07,941 --> 00:16:13,860
On 16th century trading ships, they first reached North America.

258
00:16:13,861 --> 00:16:18,100
Despite their name, Norway rats originated in Northeast Asia.

259
00:16:18,101 --> 00:16:22,640
They also reached Europe by ship and by land in about 1700,

260
00:16:22,641 --> 00:16:26,180
and sailed with colonial settlers sometime after 1755

261
00:16:26,181 --> 00:16:28,780
to North America.

262
00:16:28,781 --> 00:16:31,700
It's somewhat of a misnomer calling it a Norway rat

263
00:16:31,701 --> 00:16:34,560
because it did not originate in Norway.

264
00:16:34,561 --> 00:16:36,300
The reason it's called a Norway rat

265
00:16:36,301 --> 00:16:39,700
is because it was studied in Norway.

266
00:16:39,701 --> 00:16:42,060
Although the Norway rat arrived in the United States

267
00:16:42,061 --> 00:16:46,660
after the roof rat, it has all but taken over.

268
00:16:46,661 --> 00:16:49,540
And some estimate there could be as many as a quarter billion

269
00:16:49,541 --> 00:16:53,020
rats in the United States today.

270
00:16:53,021 --> 00:16:56,880
They can be found anywhere humans provide food and shelter,

271
00:16:56,881 --> 00:16:59,720
from fast food joints to fancy restaurants.

272
00:16:59,721 --> 00:17:03,320
The opportunistic rodent dines on our daily scraps.

273
00:17:03,321 --> 00:17:05,920
Rats are called commensal, and commensal

274
00:17:05,921 --> 00:17:08,200
means that share the table.

275
00:17:08,201 --> 00:17:11,520
And we do, in fact, share our table with the rat,

276
00:17:11,521 --> 00:17:14,760
and they can be in any type of structure.

277
00:17:14,761 --> 00:17:16,560
At times, rats have even turned up

278
00:17:16,561 --> 00:17:21,160
creeping through the corridors inside the White House itself.

279
00:17:21,161 --> 00:17:23,020
Even the greatest security systems

280
00:17:23,021 --> 00:17:25,760
can't stop this invader.

281
00:17:25,761 --> 00:17:29,840
At the core of the rat's success is its reproductive ability.

282
00:17:29,841 --> 00:17:31,940
The prolific rodent reaches sexual maturity

283
00:17:31,941 --> 00:17:35,140
at just three months and may produce up to 12 litters

284
00:17:35,141 --> 00:17:40,100
per year, each with about eight baby rats called pups.

285
00:17:40,101 --> 00:17:44,340
So after one year, a female rat could give birth to more than 100 pups.

286
00:17:45,220 --> 00:17:48,880
When her children, grandchildren, and great-grandchildren get into the act,

287
00:17:49,520 --> 00:17:55,040
it's possible for her to be responsible for up to 16,000 offspring in a single year.

288
00:17:56,300 --> 00:18:00,140
And if she survives for the rat's average lifespan of three years,

289
00:18:00,460 --> 00:18:05,100
under ideal circumstances, that number could approach 100,000.

290
00:18:06,740 --> 00:18:09,960
That means a plentiful food supply in some parts of the world.

291
00:18:10,100 --> 00:18:16,280
Because in some countries, such as India, China, and the Philippines, people eat rats.

292
00:18:17,280 --> 00:18:22,420
Keep in mind, these rats have been living out in the wild in the fields and are grain-fed

293
00:18:22,421 --> 00:18:24,060
and have not been feeding on garbage.

294
00:18:24,620 --> 00:18:27,080
But the rat can be much more than a hearty meal.

295
00:18:29,240 --> 00:18:32,600
To the Chinese, the rat is a symbol of good luck and wealth.

296
00:18:33,680 --> 00:18:37,560
And the rodent holds the honor of being the first animal on the Chinese zodiac.

297
00:18:37,561 --> 00:18:43,560
It's a very, very honored position to be in the year of the rat.

298
00:18:43,561 --> 00:18:49,560
The rat is viewed as a very cunning, very intelligent, very resourceful animal,

299
00:18:49,561 --> 00:18:53,560
and people try to emulate the rat.

300
00:18:53,561 --> 00:18:57,560
Some followers of Hinduism worship the rat.

301
00:18:57,561 --> 00:19:02,560
In fact, a temple in Deshnukh, India, houses 20,000 rats.

302
00:19:02,561 --> 00:19:04,560
But this is not an infestation.

303
00:19:04,561 --> 00:19:07,800
Legend has it that the god of death took the life of a child,

304
00:19:07,801 --> 00:19:11,400
and a good god came and restored the life of a child

305
00:19:11,401 --> 00:19:15,520
and set up rats as an intermediary for reincarnation.

306
00:19:16,100 --> 00:19:20,060
So the belief in this temple is that these rats are people

307
00:19:20,061 --> 00:19:22,060
that will be coming back to life at some other time.

308
00:19:23,820 --> 00:19:27,260
Eating food tasted by the rats is considered a supreme blessing,

309
00:19:27,760 --> 00:19:31,040
and seeing one of the rare white rats is especially holy.

310
00:19:31,041 --> 00:19:35,180
Perhaps there are other reasons for standing in awe of the rat.

311
00:19:35,181 --> 00:19:39,060
It has incredible physical abilities.

312
00:19:39,800 --> 00:19:45,500
And since 1974, rats have been showcasing some of their superhero skills

313
00:19:45,501 --> 00:19:49,220
at Nebraska Wesleyan University's Extreme Rat Challenge.

314
00:19:50,800 --> 00:19:57,160
The Extreme Rat Challenge is actually the final exam for the laboratory portion

315
00:19:57,161 --> 00:20:00,260
of our psychology class titled Learning.

316
00:20:01,040 --> 00:20:08,040
Throughout the course, each student trains a rat to compete in climbing, tightrope walking,

317
00:20:08,041 --> 00:20:13,140
hurdles, swimming, and long jump.

318
00:20:13,141 --> 00:20:17,680
We use Sprague Dolly rats, and these are white albino rats.

319
00:20:17,681 --> 00:20:23,260
So they have no pigment in their eyes and as a consequence really cannot see.

320
00:20:23,261 --> 00:20:31,440
So the students have to get used to using either olfactory cues, smells, or auditory noises.

321
00:20:32,640 --> 00:20:33,620
This is Cleo.

322
00:20:33,780 --> 00:20:37,580
She's going to be walking across the three-foot tightrope she actually placed first in this

323
00:20:37,581 --> 00:20:39,100
in the extreme rat challenge.

324
00:20:40,560 --> 00:20:41,500
Good girl.

325
00:20:44,440 --> 00:20:48,200
To succeed in this event, the rat relies on its tail for balance.

326
00:20:48,201 --> 00:20:50,680
and since rats don't sweat,

327
00:20:51,960 --> 00:20:54,220
the appendage also helps the rodents

328
00:20:54,221 --> 00:20:55,960
to keep their cool as they compete.

329
00:20:56,740 --> 00:20:59,860
By constricting or expanding blood vessels in their tails,

330
00:21:00,480 --> 00:21:02,040
rats regulate their body temperature.

331
00:21:03,440 --> 00:21:04,400
If given the chance,

332
00:21:04,820 --> 00:21:07,400
a rat could actually keep up with an Olympian runner.

333
00:21:08,400 --> 00:21:10,520
In short spurts, rats can sprint as fast

334
00:21:10,521 --> 00:21:12,580
as 24 miles per hour.

335
00:21:13,040 --> 00:21:15,420
On the hurdles, which is a total of five yards

336
00:21:15,421 --> 00:21:16,240
all the way around,

337
00:21:16,241 --> 00:21:18,940
our record is 3.78 seconds.

338
00:21:19,640 --> 00:21:21,800
And that's a rat that's really flying.

339
00:21:22,560 --> 00:21:25,500
In a newly added event, the rats take a quick dip

340
00:21:25,501 --> 00:21:27,460
and swim their way to a hidden platform.

341
00:21:28,880 --> 00:21:30,500
And that's nothing for rats.

342
00:21:31,300 --> 00:21:33,660
They can tread water for three days,

343
00:21:34,360 --> 00:21:36,040
hold their breath for three minutes,

344
00:21:36,380 --> 00:21:39,300
and swim up to a half mile if necessary.

345
00:21:40,620 --> 00:21:43,520
But perhaps the most thrilling event is the long jump.

346
00:21:43,521 --> 00:21:49,160
The longest jump on record is 48 and a half inches.

347
00:21:49,161 --> 00:21:52,460
We have had rats jump longer in practices,

348
00:21:53,080 --> 00:21:54,780
but that's been unofficial.

349
00:21:56,240 --> 00:21:59,300
A four-foot jump for a rat is like a human

350
00:21:59,301 --> 00:22:01,540
leaping across a 20-foot wide chasm,

351
00:22:02,080 --> 00:22:04,380
and that's without a running start.

352
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:08,480
The rat's awesome abilities don't stop there.

353
00:22:09,200 --> 00:22:11,680
They can slip through a hole the size of a quarter,

354
00:22:11,681 --> 00:22:17,680
scale a brick wall, and even survive a fall from up to 50 feet high.

355
00:22:18,960 --> 00:22:21,820
But no matter what the rat may do to dazzle us,

356
00:22:22,380 --> 00:22:26,640
it'll probably never escape the stigma of being a transmitter of disease.

357
00:22:27,740 --> 00:22:31,660
The rat carries about 70 diseases that are communicable to humans.

358
00:22:32,240 --> 00:22:34,720
They include bacterial diseases of the bloodstream,

359
00:22:34,980 --> 00:22:38,840
such as typhus, leptospirosis, and rat bite fever,

360
00:22:38,841 --> 00:22:42,920
as well as trichinosis, an intestinal parasitic disease.

361
00:22:44,480 --> 00:22:48,920
Rats will also carry salmonella, which is the food poisoning.

362
00:22:49,340 --> 00:22:51,840
They can walk through a contaminated food area

363
00:22:51,841 --> 00:22:54,280
and spread it on to uncontaminated food.

364
00:22:54,700 --> 00:22:58,220
And of course, one of the most famous diseases carried by the rat

365
00:22:58,221 --> 00:22:59,700
is the bubonic plague.

366
00:23:01,120 --> 00:23:05,140
In 1347, merchant ships from Central Asia

367
00:23:05,141 --> 00:23:07,580
brought plague-infected black rats to Europe.

368
00:23:07,581 --> 00:23:13,940
By the end of the 14th century, more than a third of Europe's population, or about 200

369
00:23:13,941 --> 00:23:17,460
million people, had died from the plague.

370
00:23:17,461 --> 00:23:22,920
Since the skin of those infected turned black, it became known as the Black Death.

371
00:23:22,921 --> 00:23:28,000
Some people believed that it was religious in nature, but scientists thought that it

372
00:23:28,001 --> 00:23:30,440
was tied into some sort of animal.

373
00:23:30,441 --> 00:23:35,280
They thought it might be related to the rat, but how would it be related to the rat if

374
00:23:35,281 --> 00:23:37,780
if the rats were not biting people.

375
00:23:37,781 --> 00:23:41,220
It wasn't until 1894 that scientists

376
00:23:41,221 --> 00:23:44,300
discovered the true culprit for the spread of the disease,

377
00:23:44,301 --> 00:23:46,760
the oriental rat flea.

378
00:23:46,761 --> 00:23:48,900
After feeding on black rats, the flea

379
00:23:48,901 --> 00:23:51,960
would then feed on people, thus transferring the disease

380
00:23:51,961 --> 00:23:54,700
unknowingly to humans.

381
00:23:54,701 --> 00:23:57,880
The plague died out in Europe in the mid-18th century

382
00:23:57,881 --> 00:23:59,780
for unknown reasons.

383
00:23:59,781 --> 00:24:01,480
But many attribute its disappearance

384
00:24:01,481 --> 00:24:05,460
to improve sanitation and the construction of sewer systems.

385
00:24:05,461 --> 00:24:09,020
Even so, rats can still harbor plague.

386
00:24:09,021 --> 00:24:12,080
With so many rats running amok in the United States,

387
00:24:12,081 --> 00:24:14,880
could we be at risk for a modern outbreak?

388
00:24:14,881 --> 00:24:18,120
Today, the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention

389
00:24:18,121 --> 00:24:20,980
still maintain a plague branch

390
00:24:20,981 --> 00:24:23,720
to evaluate any potential plague outbreaks.

391
00:24:23,721 --> 00:24:26,000
So this does not happen again.

392
00:24:27,040 --> 00:24:28,820
And the US government has concluded

393
00:24:28,821 --> 00:24:31,320
that rats in America do not carry the plague.

394
00:24:33,060 --> 00:24:35,940
Even so, an up-close encounter with the rat

395
00:24:35,941 --> 00:24:37,940
makes most people's skin crawl.

396
00:24:39,040 --> 00:24:42,140
But not those in more than a half a million households

397
00:24:42,141 --> 00:24:44,220
who are giving new meaning to the phrase,

398
00:24:44,221 --> 00:24:45,440
man's best friend.

399
00:24:47,700 --> 00:24:51,740
Since the 1950s, Alberta, Canada has been rat-free.

400
00:24:51,741 --> 00:24:53,980
Due to a strict rat control program,

401
00:24:53,981 --> 00:24:56,740
even pet rats are not allowed in the Canadian province.

402
00:24:56,741 --> 00:24:59,740
Rats will return on Modern Marvels.

403
00:24:59,741 --> 00:25:08,740
Agouti, Blue Burmese, Parley, Black Velveteen Dumbo,

404
00:25:08,741 --> 00:25:11,740
Dwarf, Hairless.

405
00:25:11,741 --> 00:25:13,740
These rats may all look different,

406
00:25:13,741 --> 00:25:15,740
but they have one thing in common.

407
00:25:15,741 --> 00:25:19,740
They're all so-called fancy show rats.

408
00:25:19,741 --> 00:25:22,740
And for 25 years, rats like these have strutted their stuff

409
00:25:22,741 --> 00:25:24,740
to win over the hearts of judges

410
00:25:24,741 --> 00:25:28,940
of judges at the annual American Fancy Rat and Mouse Association

411
00:25:28,941 --> 00:25:30,920
show in Riverside, California.

412
00:25:32,920 --> 00:25:34,120
It's a competition.

413
00:25:34,440 --> 00:25:38,360
We have written standards where we judge the animals on their coat,

414
00:25:38,740 --> 00:25:42,420
their color, their conformation, their size,

415
00:25:43,320 --> 00:25:46,180
all the characteristics that make up a rat,

416
00:25:46,280 --> 00:25:48,180
head, eyes, ears, tail, everything.

417
00:25:48,720 --> 00:25:51,880
Today there's 81 rats entered in all the various classes.

418
00:25:51,881 --> 00:25:58,240
Similar to a cat or dog show, there is an official standard for each type of rat.

419
00:25:59,280 --> 00:26:04,360
At the end of the day, one lucky rat receives the coveted title of best in show.

420
00:26:06,060 --> 00:26:09,280
Upon arriving, each rat must pass a rigorous health check.

421
00:26:10,380 --> 00:26:18,260
We're looking for anything that makes the rat sick that could potentially be passed on to other animals in the show.

422
00:26:18,261 --> 00:26:21,100
I'm looking for parasites, lice and mites,

423
00:26:21,101 --> 00:26:24,400
any fur missing or anything abnormal on the belly.

424
00:26:26,440 --> 00:26:29,620
All approved rats are assigned to their own show box.

425
00:26:29,900 --> 00:26:31,680
This class is stud buck,

426
00:26:32,100 --> 00:26:35,680
and basically we judge it on confirmation,

427
00:26:36,700 --> 00:26:38,880
coloring, and then markings.

428
00:26:39,760 --> 00:26:44,080
And from there we can tell which is the best male rat

429
00:26:44,081 --> 00:26:47,520
for passing his qualities on to his kids.

430
00:26:47,521 --> 00:26:53,000
and I'm just looking to see how he pleases me with his size,

431
00:26:53,180 --> 00:26:54,580
that he's not overweight.

432
00:26:55,120 --> 00:26:58,560
I look to see about his tail is a good size.

433
00:26:58,800 --> 00:27:00,500
His eyes are good.

434
00:27:01,080 --> 00:27:01,920
They're big.

435
00:27:02,260 --> 00:27:03,300
Theirs are good.

436
00:27:03,880 --> 00:27:07,080
And his head is a good-sized wedgie head.

437
00:27:08,760 --> 00:27:11,080
The judging is vigilant and precise,

438
00:27:11,540 --> 00:27:13,860
and this class is proving to be a tough call.

439
00:27:14,320 --> 00:27:16,920
I'm going to be going with this one for best stud.

440
00:27:17,520 --> 00:27:20,020
the cinnamon pearl.

441
00:27:20,021 --> 00:27:26,360
He wins on type, the condition and the coloring too.

442
00:27:26,361 --> 00:27:28,340
As winner of this class, the cinnamon pearl

443
00:27:28,341 --> 00:27:32,140
could be a top contender for best in show.

444
00:27:32,141 --> 00:27:34,440
Rat breeders come in all ages.

445
00:27:34,441 --> 00:27:38,000
I decided that I was going to start breeding hairless rats

446
00:27:38,001 --> 00:27:39,340
because there were none.

447
00:27:39,341 --> 00:27:41,180
And it was just something that I wanted to do,

448
00:27:41,181 --> 00:27:43,780
kind of an experience.

449
00:27:43,781 --> 00:27:45,640
I got my little boy out of it.

450
00:27:45,641 --> 00:27:53,800
He's a beige Rex Dumbo, but his markings disqualify him because they're too small.

451
00:27:54,000 --> 00:27:58,720
It's supposed to cover his whole belly, but see how it only covers part of his belly?

452
00:27:59,020 --> 00:28:00,620
And it's supposed to cover his whole belly.

453
00:28:00,960 --> 00:28:06,560
We live in San Diego in Point Loma, so we drive about two hours one way to come to a show.

454
00:28:07,040 --> 00:28:08,880
We have rat carriers that go everywhere.

455
00:28:10,140 --> 00:28:13,520
The house rule is that she can only house six resident rats.

456
00:28:13,521 --> 00:28:18,520
He's lovable and he's licky and who doesn't love that face?

457
00:28:18,620 --> 00:28:19,960
Look at you, yes.

458
00:28:23,260 --> 00:28:26,840
After a long day full of anticipation and rat kisses,

459
00:28:26,841 --> 00:28:28,340
it's time for Best in Show.

460
00:28:29,280 --> 00:28:33,680
This is the Best Standard and Best in Show rat.

461
00:28:33,681 --> 00:28:37,580
It's a cinnamon pearl owned by Mayumi.

462
00:28:38,900 --> 00:28:40,480
He has something.

463
00:28:40,481 --> 00:28:42,160
He's a good one.

464
00:28:42,161 --> 00:28:43,080
I got lucky.

465
00:28:43,520 --> 00:28:51,920
my boy big boy so why is this big boy considered fancy the term fancy rat is the equivalent of

466
00:28:51,921 --> 00:29:00,880
saying purebred dog and dates back to victorian england at the time england was so overrun with

467
00:29:00,881 --> 00:29:07,600
rats that a sport called rat baiting became popular captive rats were thrust into baiting

468
00:29:07,601 --> 00:29:12,100
pits by the hundreds to compete against a single dog.

469
00:29:12,101 --> 00:29:15,280
The idea was to wager which dog could

470
00:29:15,281 --> 00:29:19,080
catch the largest number of rats.

471
00:29:19,081 --> 00:29:21,780
A good rat terrier could rid the pit of 100 rats

472
00:29:21,781 --> 00:29:24,580
in about half an hour.

473
00:29:24,581 --> 00:29:27,560
Some records claim a champion named Jack Underhill

474
00:29:27,561 --> 00:29:31,360
once managed the feed in 11.5 minutes.

475
00:29:31,361 --> 00:29:35,420
That's less than seven seconds per kill.

476
00:29:35,421 --> 00:29:36,960
With the popularity of rat baiting,

477
00:29:36,961 --> 00:29:41,500
some enterprising Londoners started to take on a new profession, the rat catcher.

478
00:29:43,560 --> 00:29:47,421
One of the most famous was the royal rat catcher, Jack Black.

479
00:29:47,422 --> 00:29:49,700
While gathering rats for baiting one

480
00:29:49,701 --> 00:29:52,508
night, Black found a white rat in a graveyard.

481
00:29:52,509 --> 00:29:55,500
He captured it and decided to breed it due to its

482
00:29:55,501 --> 00:29:56,594
unique coloring.

483
00:29:56,595 --> 00:30:02,200
Jack Black was quite a celebrity for his time, and he was the official rat catcher

484
00:30:02,201 --> 00:30:03,181
for Queen Victoria.

485
00:30:03,182 --> 00:30:06,640
And if you think about it, it was really an experiment in genetics.

486
00:30:06,960 --> 00:30:12,260
Before long, carefully bred fancy rats became a fad among the privileged classes.

487
00:30:14,200 --> 00:30:17,600
Today, the rat fancy has societies all over the world,

488
00:30:17,820 --> 00:30:20,780
and the popularity of the pet rat continues to grow.

489
00:30:23,360 --> 00:30:25,940
At Package Rats in Southern California,

490
00:30:26,820 --> 00:30:32,520
home hobby breeder Rachel Miller raises rats to be both show-quality animals and lovable pets.

491
00:30:33,100 --> 00:30:34,680
This is my rat room.

492
00:30:34,681 --> 00:30:38,680
My roommate Adam built it especially for the rats.

493
00:30:38,681 --> 00:30:41,680
And over here we have the boys' area.

494
00:30:41,681 --> 00:30:44,680
I have seven boys, and they have their little play area

495
00:30:44,681 --> 00:30:47,680
where they come out and play a couple hours a day.

496
00:30:47,681 --> 00:30:49,680
They have toys just like cats and dogs,

497
00:30:49,681 --> 00:30:52,680
and they enjoy sleeping in their hammocks

498
00:30:52,681 --> 00:30:55,680
and hiding in their little hidey holes.

499
00:30:55,681 --> 00:30:58,680
This next area is the nursery.

500
00:30:58,681 --> 00:31:00,680
I have two litters right now.

501
00:31:00,681 --> 00:31:03,680
And then this is the girls' area.

502
00:31:03,681 --> 00:31:08,560
So they are asleep right now, but I do let them out during the day, and they stay out

503
00:31:08,561 --> 00:31:12,360
for many hours, so we'll go ahead and see if they'll wake up.

504
00:31:14,040 --> 00:31:18,640
Once awakened, it's easier to see why so many have adopted the alleged dirty rat into

505
00:31:18,641 --> 00:31:19,341
their family.

506
00:31:20,580 --> 00:31:25,280
A lot of people think rats are dirty, but rats are actually probably cleaner than us.

507
00:31:25,360 --> 00:31:28,740
They're constantly cleaning themselves and trying to keep neat.

508
00:31:28,800 --> 00:31:32,580
When you touch them, after you, like, go away for a few seconds, they'll start cleaning

509
00:31:32,581 --> 00:31:35,080
themselves because they're like, yeah, human's on me.

510
00:31:35,081 --> 00:31:37,120
Gross.

511
00:31:37,121 --> 00:31:40,980
In fact, rats bathe themselves six times a day or more.

512
00:31:40,981 --> 00:31:43,280
Talk about fancy.

513
00:31:43,281 --> 00:31:46,900
But some kept rats aren't pets.

514
00:31:46,901 --> 00:31:49,120
They're laboratory animals.

515
00:31:49,121 --> 00:31:52,020
The rodent that was once a condemned plague carrier

516
00:31:52,021 --> 00:31:55,640
has been transformed into an indispensable tool for science.

517
00:31:58,840 --> 00:32:02,520
The oldest rat on record lived for seven years and four months.

518
00:32:02,521 --> 00:32:06,020
That's almost three times the lifespan of the average rat.

519
00:32:06,021 --> 00:32:08,400
Rats will return on Modern Marvels.

520
00:32:11,160 --> 00:32:12,000
The Lab Rat.

521
00:32:13,760 --> 00:32:16,400
Every day, thousands work their tails off

522
00:32:16,401 --> 00:32:17,840
in the real rat race.

523
00:32:18,760 --> 00:32:21,240
Looks like a typical day in the maze, right?

524
00:32:21,241 --> 00:32:22,500
Well, not quite.

525
00:32:22,501 --> 00:32:24,400
When people think about rats and mazes,

526
00:32:24,401 --> 00:32:26,840
a lot of times they imagine those types of mazes

527
00:32:26,841 --> 00:32:30,480
with lots of right and left and wrong turns and dead ends.

528
00:32:30,481 --> 00:32:33,060
And that's not really what we use in research these days.

529
00:32:35,540 --> 00:32:38,220
At the UCLA Behavioral Testing Corps,

530
00:32:39,020 --> 00:32:41,520
rats are put to the test in one of three standard mazes.

531
00:32:42,460 --> 00:32:43,700
The Morris Water Maze,

532
00:32:44,320 --> 00:32:45,120
the T-Maze,

533
00:32:45,840 --> 00:32:47,700
and the Radial Arm Maze.

534
00:32:49,500 --> 00:32:52,660
Rat mazes have helped experts study the principles of human learning

535
00:32:52,661 --> 00:32:57,400
and determine how different drugs, treatments, or conditions affect memory.

536
00:32:57,401 --> 00:33:06,350
And the reason that we look at them in rats is because the rat brain and the human brain are very similar in terms of how new memories are formed and also how we

537
00:33:06,351 --> 00:33:08,240
navigate in a spatial environment.

538
00:33:09,680 --> 00:33:14,300
But when it comes to navigation for the rat, vision plays almost no part.

539
00:33:15,040 --> 00:33:17,160
That's because rats are extremely nearsighted.

540
00:33:18,860 --> 00:33:25,460
In fact, studies suggest their vision may be something like this, black and white, and only about 2,600.

541
00:33:25,461 --> 00:33:29,460
That means they can only see objects within four feet,

542
00:33:29,461 --> 00:33:33,460
while everything beyond would be too blurry to distinguish.

543
00:33:33,461 --> 00:33:36,460
But that doesn't stop the resolute rodent.

544
00:33:36,461 --> 00:33:39,460
Rats have a very highly developed sense of touch,

545
00:33:39,461 --> 00:33:42,460
similar to human fingertips, but they use it through their

546
00:33:42,461 --> 00:33:43,460
whisker tips.

547
00:33:43,461 --> 00:33:46,460
And so they stay up against the wall,

548
00:33:46,461 --> 00:33:49,460
or they keep their whiskers brushing the floor to help them

549
00:33:49,461 --> 00:33:51,460
get around.

550
00:33:51,461 --> 00:33:53,460
Of course, rats need some sort of incentive

551
00:33:53,461 --> 00:33:56,120
of incentive to solve problems.

552
00:33:56,220 --> 00:33:59,600
And in this case, it's peanut butter Captain Crunch.

553
00:33:59,700 --> 00:34:02,860
So this is the radial arm maze.

554
00:34:02,960 --> 00:34:05,940
It's used to take advantage of natural foraging behavior

555
00:34:06,040 --> 00:34:06,780
in rodents.

556
00:34:09,580 --> 00:34:13,040
The animal should go down an arm, gather the food at one end,

557
00:34:13,140 --> 00:34:14,840
and then it should move to another arm

558
00:34:14,940 --> 00:34:18,780
before it returns back to the same arm again.

559
00:34:18,880 --> 00:34:20,720
Perhaps one of the reasons rats have a knack

560
00:34:20,721 --> 00:34:25,660
for the maze is their ability to follow their own scent trail.

561
00:34:25,760 --> 00:34:27,420
So researchers turn to another maze

562
00:34:27,520 --> 00:34:29,760
to eliminate the odor cue.

563
00:34:29,860 --> 00:34:31,460
This is the Morris Water Maze task.

564
00:34:31,560 --> 00:34:33,260
And in this task, the goal of the rat

565
00:34:33,360 --> 00:34:35,660
is to find a hidden platform that's

566
00:34:35,760 --> 00:34:39,680
just under the surface of the water.

567
00:34:39,780 --> 00:34:41,380
At first, the rat swims aimlessly

568
00:34:41,480 --> 00:34:42,580
in search of dry land.

569
00:34:45,880 --> 00:34:48,780
But after just five trials, he has no problem

570
00:34:48,880 --> 00:34:50,180
finding the platform.

571
00:34:50,181 --> 00:34:52,540
This is really a test of spatial navigation.

572
00:34:53,160 --> 00:34:57,280
So how well the rat can navigate in space with just visual cues is a guideline.

573
00:34:58,280 --> 00:35:02,060
The rat maze may provide information on learning, emotion, and memory.

574
00:35:04,000 --> 00:35:07,520
But the lab rat has contributed so much more to human well-being.

575
00:35:09,260 --> 00:35:12,820
While mice have become the leading research animal in the United States,

576
00:35:13,860 --> 00:35:16,920
labs still rely on about 20 million rats every year.

577
00:35:16,921 --> 00:35:23,640
The Medical College of Wisconsin is one of the few institutions where rats reign supreme.

578
00:35:24,780 --> 00:35:28,380
Our most valuable possessions besides our people is our rat colony.

579
00:35:28,960 --> 00:35:34,780
We currently house about 6,000 rats per day at a cost of about 50 cents per rat per day.

580
00:35:35,220 --> 00:35:42,000
That's more than a million dollars a year just to house the rodents by day and night.

581
00:35:43,040 --> 00:35:45,920
So why spend so much time and money on rats?

582
00:35:46,920 --> 00:35:51,360
A researcher can study things in the rat's lifespan of just three years

583
00:35:51,361 --> 00:35:55,160
that would take the average lifespan of 70 years to observe in humans.

584
00:35:56,760 --> 00:36:01,520
That's because rats suffer the same diseases and grow old much as humans do,

585
00:36:02,080 --> 00:36:04,060
just some 20 times faster.

586
00:36:04,900 --> 00:36:10,200
At the Medical College of Wisconsin, we focus on using the rat in a couple of key areas.

587
00:36:10,500 --> 00:36:14,140
Diabetes, high blood pressure, heart attacks, and obesity,

588
00:36:14,141 --> 00:36:18,960
which are some of the most common forms of disease found in humans.

589
00:36:20,300 --> 00:36:24,060
Thanks to the rat, millions of human lives have been saved.

590
00:36:24,840 --> 00:36:29,340
The reliable rodents were on the front lines in the development of organ transplants,

591
00:36:30,000 --> 00:36:34,740
the polio vaccine, antidepressants, magnetic resonance imaging,

592
00:36:35,300 --> 00:36:39,400
artificial organ tissue, and cholesterol regulation, just to name a few.

593
00:36:39,401 --> 00:36:46,400
But does the march of medical science justify taking the lives of so many rats?

594
00:36:47,400 --> 00:36:50,620
Having to kill an animal is a difficult part of the job.

595
00:36:51,140 --> 00:36:54,800
I wish we were at a point where we actually didn't have to use animal models.

596
00:36:55,220 --> 00:36:58,860
It's just not practical with our knowledge base to enable that to happen.

597
00:37:00,520 --> 00:37:01,920
At least not yet.

598
00:37:02,560 --> 00:37:03,620
But we're getting closer.

599
00:37:03,621 --> 00:37:09,860
In March 2004, scientists sequenced the entire genome of the brown Norway rat.

600
00:37:10,640 --> 00:37:16,600
With more research, this may enable the creation of computer-generated models to replace most lab rats.

601
00:37:18,780 --> 00:37:24,280
The rat genome revealed that rats are about 90% identical to humans at the genetic level.

602
00:37:24,780 --> 00:37:30,620
So we can find the disease gene in the rat, and now because we're so similar to the rat,

603
00:37:30,621 --> 00:37:33,800
take that information using simply computers,

604
00:37:34,260 --> 00:37:35,740
find out where it is in the human,

605
00:37:35,741 --> 00:37:38,240
and then go test a clinical population

606
00:37:38,241 --> 00:37:41,720
to see if the same gene causes the same effect in humans.

607
00:37:43,140 --> 00:37:45,300
The genome also allows scientists

608
00:37:45,301 --> 00:37:47,400
to insert foreign genes into the rat.

609
00:37:48,520 --> 00:37:51,060
Many of the so-called transgenic rats

610
00:37:51,061 --> 00:37:54,200
contain artificial genes to trigger human disease.

611
00:37:55,760 --> 00:37:57,820
But at the Medical College of Wisconsin,

612
00:37:57,821 --> 00:38:02,820
Researchers have put a new twist on transgenics.

613
00:38:02,821 --> 00:38:05,820
It started with the DNA from a jellyfish.

614
00:38:05,821 --> 00:38:10,820
More specifically, the DNA that encodes for green fluorescent protein in the marine animal.

615
00:38:10,821 --> 00:38:17,820
We then injected this DNA into a one-cell stage rat embryo.

616
00:38:17,821 --> 00:38:24,820
And then this embryo would transfer back to the uterus of the foster mother.

617
00:38:24,821 --> 00:38:28,760
And 20 days later, transgenic pups.

618
00:38:30,100 --> 00:38:32,560
Both of these pups may seem normal at first,

619
00:38:32,920 --> 00:38:35,860
but under a blue light and through a yellow filter,

620
00:38:36,560 --> 00:38:39,640
you can clearly see the jellyfish protein in the pup on the right.

621
00:38:40,600 --> 00:38:42,240
And this still-beating heart,

622
00:38:42,600 --> 00:38:45,080
just surgically removed from an adult transgenic rat,

623
00:38:45,680 --> 00:38:47,800
clearly shows how every bit of the rat's DNA

624
00:38:47,801 --> 00:38:49,440
contains the green fluorescence.

625
00:38:51,020 --> 00:38:54,480
The glow-in-the-dark rat represents more than scientific oddity.

626
00:38:54,820 --> 00:38:57,820
When we're developing new technologies for modifying

627
00:38:57,920 --> 00:39:02,120
the rat genome, we often use the green fluorescent protein

628
00:39:02,220 --> 00:39:05,760
as a test bed for the simple reason that all we have to do

629
00:39:05,860 --> 00:39:08,700
is look in the pups with this light and see whether or not

630
00:39:08,800 --> 00:39:11,900
the experiment worked.

631
00:39:12,000 --> 00:39:15,340
To millions, the lab rat is already a hero.

632
00:39:15,440 --> 00:39:17,440
But how about a rescue rat rummaging

633
00:39:17,540 --> 00:39:20,240
through the rubble of a collapsed building?

634
00:39:20,340 --> 00:39:23,880
Say hello to the robo-rat.

635
00:39:23,881 --> 00:39:28,680
The rat genome revealed that rats have been evolving about three times faster than humans.

636
00:39:29,960 --> 00:39:32,280
Rats will return on Modern Marvels.

637
00:39:34,980 --> 00:39:36,740
Meet Peekaboo the rat.

638
00:39:37,860 --> 00:39:40,620
Place her in front of any challenge and she won't hesitate.

639
00:39:42,460 --> 00:39:44,960
But that's because she's no ordinary rat.

640
00:39:46,420 --> 00:39:51,720
This live rat is actually being remotely controlled to go right, left or straight.

641
00:39:53,880 --> 00:39:55,500
Sounds like science fiction, right?

642
00:39:56,340 --> 00:39:59,740
But at the State University of New York College of Medicine in Brooklyn,

643
00:40:00,600 --> 00:40:03,020
scientists have made the robo-rat a reality.

644
00:40:04,280 --> 00:40:08,180
And rats like peekaboo may one day become the ultimate rescue animal.

645
00:40:08,840 --> 00:40:13,320
We started this work just before the events of 9-11.

646
00:40:13,980 --> 00:40:20,260
And when the towers fell, it really caused us to think a lot about search and rescue.

647
00:40:20,261 --> 00:40:25,240
A rat is small enough to be able to get through the very small spaces

648
00:40:25,241 --> 00:40:28,460
to find people that are under the rubble piles.

649
00:40:29,980 --> 00:40:36,280
For decades, rescue teams have relied on dogs to locate buried victims of natural or man-made disasters.

650
00:40:37,600 --> 00:40:41,840
But some believe the rat may be better equipped to take on this dangerous challenge.

651
00:40:43,320 --> 00:40:47,140
That's because the rat's sense of smell is just as powerful as a dog's,

652
00:40:47,600 --> 00:40:50,020
and it can venture where no dog ever could.

653
00:40:50,260 --> 00:40:56,980
We have actually demonstrated that rats can be trained to find explosives.

654
00:40:57,600 --> 00:41:03,200
Think about, for example, if there is a bomb somewhere in a school or in an airport.

655
00:41:03,860 --> 00:41:08,000
Typically, you'd have a dog and a trainer go into that space.

656
00:41:08,320 --> 00:41:10,080
That's a little bit of a dangerous profession.

657
00:41:10,320 --> 00:41:16,580
But think about it instead as something where the rat is going into this dangerous space.

658
00:41:16,581 --> 00:41:23,600
Many criticize the ethics of creating a cyborg rat and assert the experiment is cruel.

659
00:41:24,920 --> 00:41:28,620
But Dr. Chapin insists the rat is almost completely unaware of it.

660
00:41:29,560 --> 00:41:33,960
The implants in the animal's head are not felt by the rat.

661
00:41:35,400 --> 00:41:39,320
Chapin also contends the stimulations used to guide the rat are not harmful.

662
00:41:40,620 --> 00:41:42,960
In fact, he says they're actually pleasurable.

663
00:41:42,961 --> 00:41:48,840
We put in an electrode into the animal's reward center.

664
00:41:49,520 --> 00:41:55,120
The ability to reward the animal with the treat over and over again conditions the animal

665
00:41:55,121 --> 00:41:58,060
so that it gets better and better at it as time goes on.

666
00:41:58,320 --> 00:42:01,460
So that's effectively the exact same thing that we're doing here,

667
00:42:01,540 --> 00:42:05,540
except that we're substituting an actual physical reward

668
00:42:05,541 --> 00:42:10,220
and converting it directly into the brain's center for reward.

669
00:42:10,221 --> 00:42:14,220
So just how did the robo-rat first come about?

670
00:42:14,221 --> 00:42:20,220
We are really interested in spinal cord injury in human beings.

671
00:42:20,221 --> 00:42:23,220
So we started by doing some rat studies.

672
00:42:23,221 --> 00:42:28,220
We wanted to find out whether if you put electrodes into the brain of a rat,

673
00:42:28,221 --> 00:42:31,220
specifically in the somatosensory cortex,

674
00:42:31,221 --> 00:42:35,220
that's the part of your cortex that is responsible for the sense of touch.

675
00:42:35,221 --> 00:42:38,220
Does the rat really feel it?

676
00:42:38,221 --> 00:42:44,140
To test their theory, Chapin and his team implanted an electrode in each side of the

677
00:42:44,141 --> 00:42:51,980
rat's somatosensory cortex, one to stimulate the right whiskers and one for the left.

678
00:42:51,981 --> 00:42:58,680
By tapping a keyboard up to 100 meters away, RoboRat operator Shawa Shu sends signals via

679
00:42:58,681 --> 00:43:01,180
radio waves to the implanted electrodes.

680
00:43:01,181 --> 00:43:08,180
Left. Right. Left.

681
00:43:08,181 --> 00:43:11,180
We know that that will produce a sensation,

682
00:43:11,181 --> 00:43:14,180
probably like a tingling on his whiskers.

683
00:43:14,181 --> 00:43:16,180
Then the animal is supposed to turn.

684
00:43:16,181 --> 00:43:20,180
If he turns correctly, then Xiaowa will press the button

685
00:43:20,181 --> 00:43:22,180
that says reward.

686
00:43:22,181 --> 00:43:25,180
And that happens in less than a second.

687
00:43:25,181 --> 00:43:27,180
That's so the rat knows it's being rewarded

688
00:43:27,181 --> 00:43:30,180
for following the commands.

689
00:43:30,181 --> 00:43:34,100
All the tiny electronics necessary to drive the rodent are carried right on its back.

690
00:43:34,920 --> 00:43:39,400
We also have a little camera on top of the rat,

691
00:43:39,620 --> 00:43:43,140
so it allows us to be able to see where the rat is going.

692
00:43:44,960 --> 00:43:49,760
When or if rats will someday rescue us from the rubble remains to be seen.

693
00:43:50,480 --> 00:43:53,300
Still, that probably won't change the rat's bad rap.

694
00:43:54,440 --> 00:43:56,060
That is up to us.

695
00:43:56,061 --> 00:44:01,060
So the next time you see a rat, will you quiver at the sight of the villainous Berman,

696
00:44:01,061 --> 00:44:05,060
or embrace the possibility of a rodent savior?

697
00:44:05,061 --> 00:44:09,060
Either way, the contradictory rat is here to stay.

698
00:44:09,061 --> 00:44:14,060
There have been plenty of stories that the rats and the cockroaches will be the last survivors.

699
00:44:14,061 --> 00:44:19,060
I don't think we'll ever know that for sure, but I wouldn't bet against the rat.

